Sec. 90001 appropriates $46.55 billion to the Commissioner of U.S. Customs and Border Protection for FY2025 (available through September 30, 2029) to construct, install, or improve primary, waterborne, and secondary barriers; access roads; barrier system attributes such as cameras, lights, and sensors; and any ground-preparation work needed for barrier construction and maintenance.

Sec. 90002 appropriates to the CBP Commissioner for FY2025 (through September 30, 2029): $4.1 billion to hire and train Border Patrol agents, Office of Field Operations officers, Air and Marine agents, rehired annuitants, and field support personnel; $2.05263 billion for recruitment bonuses, performance awards, and retention bonuses; $855 million to repair existing and lease or acquire additional patrol vehicles; and $5 billion for leasing, acquiring, constructing, designing, or improving CBP facilities and checkpoints. None of the funds may be used to recruit, hire, or train processing coordinators after October 31, 2028.

Sec. 90003 appropriates $45 billion to U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement for FY2025 (through September 30, 2029) for single adult alien detention capacity and family residential center capacity. Aliens may be detained at family residential centers pending removal decisions and until removal; detention standards for single adult capacity are set at the Secretary of Homeland Security's discretion, consistent with law. A "family residential center" is defined as a DHS facility detaining family units (including alien children who are not unaccompanied alien children under 6 U.S.C. 279(g)).

Sec. 90004 appropriates $6.168 billion to the CBP Commissioner for FY2025 (through September 30, 2029) for nonintrusive inspection equipment and civil works (including AI and machine learning) to combat narcotics at ports of entry and along the southwest, northern, and maritime borders; Air and Marine platform upgrades and procurement; border surveillance technology upgrades; the biometric entry-exit system under 8 U.S.C. 1365b; screening of persons entering or exiting; initial screenings of unaccompanied alien children consistent with the William Wilberforce Trafficking Victims Protection Reauthorization Act of 2008; combating drug trafficking including fentanyl and precursors; and commemorating border security efforts. Funds may not procure or deploy untested, unaccepted surveillance towers along the southwest or northern border for autonomous capabilities, defined as systems using AI, machine learning, or computer vision to detect, identify, classify, and track items in real time without active personnel engagement or continuous human control.

Sec. 90005 appropriates to the FEMA Administrator for FY2025 (through September 30, 2029) under the State Homeland Security Grant Program: $500 million for State and local counter-unmanned aircraft systems capabilities; $625 million for 2026 FIFA World Cup security costs; $1 billion for 2028 Olympics security, planning, and other costs; and $450 million for Operation Stonegarden. The World Cup and Olympics funds are exempt from the matching and other requirements of 6 U.S.C. 605(e)(1) and 609(a)(12). It also establishes the State Border Security Reinforcement Fund with a $10 billion FY2025 appropriation (available through September 30, 2034) for grants to eligible States and local governments for border wall/fencing/buoys construction, ground preparation, detection and interdiction of illicit substances and criminal aliens with transfer to DHS, and relocation of unlawfully present aliens from small population centers; grants may cover activities on or after January 20, 2021, applications must be accepted within 90 days of enactment, and FEMA may use up to 1 percent for administration.

Sec. 90006 appropriates $300 million to the FEMA Administrator for FY2025 (through September 30, 2029) to reimburse State and local agencies for extraordinary law enforcement personnel costs for protection activities directly and demonstrably associated with a President's residence designated under section 3 or 4 of the Presidential Protection Assistance Act of 1976 to be secured by the Secret Service. Funds cover costs incurred on or after July 1, 2024, that exceed typical law enforcement operation costs, are directly attributable to such protection, are associated with a nongovernmental designated property, and are certified as compensating activities requested by the Secret Service; FEMA may use up to 3 percent for administration.

Sec. 90007 appropriates $10 billion to the Secretary of Homeland Security for FY2025 (through September 30, 2029) for reimbursement of costs incurred in activities supporting DHS's mission to safeguard U.S. borders.

Sec. 90101, the "FEHB Protection Act of 2025," requires the Office of Personnel Management Director, within 1 year of enactment, to issue regulations and implement a process verifying qualifying life events and that added family members are qualifying family members under the Federal Employees Health Benefits Program. The Director must include ineligibles in fraud risk assessments and conduct a comprehensive 3-year audit (beginning 1 year after enactment) of covered family members, reviewing marriage and birth certificates and other documents. Within 180 days of enactment, the Director must develop a process to disenroll or remove ineligible individuals. It also amends 5 U.S.C. 8909 to exempt amounts set aside under subsection (b)(2) from annual congressional limitations and appropriates $66 million in FY2026 (available through FY2035) from contributions for OPM to carry out subsections (c) through (f).

Sec. 90102 appropriates $88 million for FY2026 (available until expended) to the Pandemic Response Accountability Committee to support oversight of the Coronavirus response and of funds provided in this Act or other Acts pertaining to the pandemic. It amends section 15010 of the CARES Act to add this reconciliation Act to the Committee's oversight jurisdiction and extends the Committee's authorization from 2025 to 2034.

Sec. 90103 appropriates $100 million to the Office of Management and Budget for FY2025 (through September 30, 2029) for purposes of finding budget and accounting efficiencies in the executive branch. [end of text]


